', '[{"input": "response(''Does this cryogenic chamber make me look fat?'')", "output": "''Sure.''"}, {"input": "response(''WATCH OUT!'')", "output": "''Whoa, chill out!''"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[{"stdin": "response(''Does this cryogenic chamber make me look fat?'')", "expected_output": "''Sure.''"}, {"stdin": "response(''WATCH OUT!'')", "expected_output": "''Whoa, chill out!''"}, {"stdin": "response(\"WHAT''S GOING ON?\")", "expected_output": "\"Calm down, I know what I''m doing!\""}, {"stdin": "response('''')", "expected_output": "''Fine. Be that way!''"}, {"stdin": "response(''Tom-ay-to, tom-aaaah-to.'')", "expected_output": "''Whatever.''"}, {"stdin": "response(''You are, what, like 15?'')", "expected_output": "''Sure.''"}, {"stdin": "response(''fffbbcbeab?'')", "expected_output": "''Sure.''"}, {"stdin": "response(''4?'')", "expected_output": "''Sure.''"}, {"stdin": "response('':) ?'')", "expected_output": "''Sure.''"}, {"stdin": "response(''Wait! Hang on. Are you going to be OK?'')", "expected_output": "''Sure.''"}, {"stdin": "response(''Okay if like my  spacebar  quite a bit?   '')", "expected_output": "''Sure.''"}, {"stdin": "response(''\\nDoes this cryogenic chamber make\\n me look fat?'')", "expected_output": "''Sure.''"}, {"stdin": "response(''FCECDFCAAB'')", "expected_output": "''Whoa, chill out!''"}, {"stdin": "response(''DO LIONS EAT PEOPLE? AHHHHH.'')", "expected_output": "''Whoa, chill out!''"}, {"stdin": "response(''1, 2, 3 GO!'')", "expected_output": "''Whoa, chill out!''"}, {"stdin": "response(''ZOMG THE %^*@#$(*^ ZOMBIES ARE COMING!!11!!1!'')", "expected_output": "''Whoa, chill out!''"}, {"stdin": "response(''I HATE THE DENTIST'')", "expected_output": "''Whoa, chill out!''"}, {"stdin": "response(''          '')", "expected_output": "''Fine. Be that way!''"}, {"stdin": "response(''\\t\\t\\t\\t\\t\\t\\t\\t\\t\\t'')", "expected_output": "''Fine. Be that way!''"}, {"stdin": "response(''\\n\\r \\t'')", "expected_output": "''Fine. Be that way!''"}, {"stdin": "response(''Hi there!'')", "expected_output": "''Whatever.''"}, {"stdin": "response(\"It''s OK if you don''t want to go work for NASA.\")", "expected_output": "''Whatever.''"}, {"stdin": "response(''1, 2, 3'')", "expected_output": "''Whatever.''"}, {"stdin": "response(''Ending with ? means a question.'')", "expected_output": "''Whatever.''"}, {"stdin": "response(''         hmmmmmmm...'')", "expected_output": "''Whatever.''"}, {"stdin": "response(''This is a statement ending with whitespace      '')", "expected_output": "''Whatever.''"}]'::jsonb, '{"source": "exercism/python@6105f6d9d3ef66ce3d5c8f529d2882af3d76b706", "slug": "bob", "license": "MIT"}'::jsonb);
INSERT INTO challenges (printed_number, title, description, difficulty, assessed_skill_id, starter_code, examples, hints, hidden_tests, judge_config) VALUES (12, 'Raindrops', '# Introduction

Raindrops is a slightly more complex version of the FizzBuzz challenge, a classic interview question.

# Instructions

Your task is to convert a number into its corresponding raindrop sounds.

If a given number:

- is divisible by 3, add "Pling" to the result.
- is divisible by 5, add "Plang" to the result.
- is divisible by 7, add "Plong" to the result.
- **is not** divisible by 3, 5, or 7, the result should be the number as a string.

## Examples

- 28 is divisible by 7, but not 3 or 5, so the result would be `"Plong"`.
- 30 is divisible by 3 and 5, but not 7, so the result would be `"PlingPlang"`.
- 34 is not divisible by 3, 5, or 7, so the result would be `"34"`.

~~~~exercism/note
A common way to test if one number is evenly divisible by another is to compare the [remainder][remainder] or [modulus][modulo] to zero.
Most languages provide operators or functions for one (or both) of these.

[remainder]: https://exercism.org/docs/programming/operators/remainder
[modulo]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modulo_operation
~~~~

# Instructions append

## How this Exercise is Structured in Python

This exercise is best solved with Python''s `%` ([modulo][modulo]) operator, which returns the remainder of positive integer division.
It has a method equivalent, `operator.mod()` in the [operator module][operator-mod].


Python also offers additional ''remainder'' methods in the [math module][math-module].
[`math.fmod()`][fmod] behaves like `%`, but operates on floats.
[`math.remainder()`][remainder] implements a "step closest to zero" algorithm for the remainder of division.
While we encourage you to get familiar with these methods, neither of these will exactly match the result of `%`, and are not recommended for use with this exercise.

The built-in function [`divmod()`][divmod] will also give a remainder than matches `%` if used with two positive integers, but returns a `tuple` that needs to be unpacked.

[divmod]: https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#divmod
[fmod]: https://docs.python.org/3/library/math.html#math.fmod
[math-module]: https://docs.python.org/3/library/math.html
[modulo]: https://www.programiz.com/python-programming/operators#arithmetic
[operator-mod]: https://docs.python.org/3/library/operator.html#operator.mod
[remainder]: https://docs.python.org/3/library/math.html#math.remainder', 'easy', 'conditionals', 'def convert(number):
