if isinstance(result, float) and result.is_integer():
    result = int(result)  # 6 / 2 gives 6.0; the answer 6 is still right
print(repr(result))
', '[{"input": "sum_of_multiples(1, [3, 5])", "output": "0"}, {"input": "sum_of_multiples(4, [3, 5])", "output": "3"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[{"stdin": "sum_of_multiples(1, [3, 5])", "expected_output": "0"}, {"stdin": "sum_of_multiples(4, [3, 5])", "expected_output": "3"}, {"stdin": "sum_of_multiples(7, [3])", "expected_output": "9"}, {"stdin": "sum_of_multiples(10, [3, 5])", "expected_output": "23"}, {"stdin": "sum_of_multiples(100, [3, 5])", "expected_output": "2318"}, {"stdin": "sum_of_multiples(1000, [3, 5])", "expected_output": "233168"}, {"stdin": "sum_of_multiples(20, [7, 13, 17])", "expected_output": "51"}, {"stdin": "sum_of_multiples(15, [4, 6])", "expected_output": "30"}, {"stdin": "sum_of_multiples(150, [5, 6, 8])", "expected_output": "4419"}, {"stdin": "sum_of_multiples(51, [5, 25])", "expected_output": "275"}, {"stdin": "sum_of_multiples(10000, [43, 47])", "expected_output": "2203160"}, {"stdin": "sum_of_multiples(100, [1])", "expected_output": "4950"}, {"stdin": "sum_of_multiples(10000, [])", "expected_output": "0"}, {"stdin": "sum_of_multiples(1, [0])", "expected_output": "0"}, {"stdin": "sum_of_multiples(4, [3, 0])", "expected_output": "3"}, {"stdin": "sum_of_multiples(10000, [2, 3, 5, 7, 11])", "expected_output": "39614537"}]'::jsonb, '{"source": "exercism/python@6105f6d9d3ef66ce3d5c8f529d2882af3d76b706", "slug": "sum-of-multiples", "license": "MIT"}'::jsonb);
INSERT INTO challenges (printed_number, title, description, difficulty, assessed_skill_id, starter_code, examples, hints, hidden_tests, judge_config) VALUES (33, 'Two Fer', '# Introduction

In some English accents, when you say "two for" quickly, it sounds like "two fer".
Two-for-one is a way of saying that if you buy one, you also get one for free.
So the phrase "two-fer" often implies a two-for-one offer.

Imagine a bakery that has a holiday offer where you can buy two cookies for the price of one ("two-fer one!").
You take the offer and (very generously) decide to give the extra cookie to someone else in the queue.

# Instructions

Your task is to determine what you will say as you give away the extra cookie.

If you know the person''s name (e.g. if they''re named Do-yun), then you will say:

```text
One for Do-yun, one for me.
```

If you don''t know the person''s name, you will say _you_ instead.

```text
One for you, one for me.
```

Here are some examples:

| Name   | Dialogue                    |
| :----- | :-------------------------- |
| Alice  | One for Alice, one for me.  |
| Bohdan | One for Bohdan, one for me. |
|        | One for you, one for me.    |
| Zaphod | One for Zaphod, one for me. |', 'easy', 'functions', 'def two_fer(name):
    pass


